-- DICT-SEMANTICS §1/§3 at a TWO-ENTRY `requires`, with the MULTI-PARAMETER
-- predicate written FIRST -- the ordering its sibling
-- s3-nary-requires-multi-entry.mdk deliberately could not use.
--
-- RE-POINTED DRAINED PIN. This program is
-- test/must_fail_fixtures/1154-multi-entry-requires-decl-order-decides/main.mdk
-- verbatim; it printed 116 there and the fixture pinned that. It drained on
-- 2026-08-23 (slice S-obligation-nary-payload) and is kept here as the cheapest
-- regression test the corpus has, per [G-PIN-DRAIN].
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived from §1/§3, NOT captured from any engine, and
-- identical to the sibling's for the same reasons -- the two files differ ONLY
-- in the order of the `requires` entries, which changes no program meaning):
--   `impl Sh (Box a) requires Ix a Char, Dbg2 a` pins the second `Ix`
--   parameter to `Char`. At the goal `Sh (Box Int)` the head match binds
--   a ↦ Int, so that obligation is the ground predicate `Ix Int Char`. §3's
--   matching set is ONE φ against the WHOLE vector: φ(`Ix Int Bool`) would need
--   Bool = Char, so the set is the singleton {`impl Ix Int Char`} -- 222.
--   `Dbg2 a` at a ↦ Int gives `dbg2 5` = 5. 222 + 5 = 227.
--
-- THE WRONG ANSWER THIS ROW CATCHES IS 116 (= 111, the `Ix Int Bool` body, + 5).
-- Declaration order of the two `impl Ix` blocks is WRONG-ANSWER-FIRST on
-- purpose, and Section 4 reverses them and asserts the answer does not move.
--
-- 🚨 WHAT THE DEFECT ACTUALLY WAS, because the obvious reading was wrong and
-- cost a slice to correct: NOT the goal vector, and NOT the routing ladder. The
-- routes handed to the call site were already correct at every position (the
-- typed Core IR dump showed `(RKey "Ix|Int Char|") (RKey "Int")`, in `requires`
-- order). The loss was on the CONSUMPTION side: `registerReqSlots` opens one
-- dict slot per `requires` ENTRY but keys `activeDictVars` on the tyvar ids
-- INSIDE the entry, so `Ix a Char, Dbg2 a` registered `a`'s id TWICE, and
-- `firstDictForEncl` scanned a cons-list whose head is the LAST registration.
-- Every method use in the body therefore read the LAST slot -- `sh` lowered to
-- `CMethod "ix" (RDict "$dict_sh_1")` beside `CMethod "dbg2" (RDict
-- "$dict_sh_1")`, with `$dict_sh_0` bound and never read. That is why the
-- LAST `requires` entry was the only one that came out right, and why moving
-- `Ix` to the end of the clause (the sibling file) hid the bug. Fixed by
-- `implReqDictVarOf`: an impl-`requires` site is resolved by its PREDICATE, the
-- rule #1177 established one channel over.
interface Ix a b where
  ix : a -> b -> Int

impl Ix Int Bool where
  ix _ _ = 111

impl Ix Int Char where
  ix _ _ = 222

interface Dbg2 a where
  dbg2 : a -> Int

impl Dbg2 Int where
  dbg2 n = n

data Box a = MkBox a

interface Sh a where
  sh : a -> Int

impl Sh (Box a) requires Ix a Char, Dbg2 a where
  sh (MkBox x) = ix x 'z' + dbg2 x

main = println (sh (MkBox 5))
